THE **`STREAM` MODULE** IN NODE.JS IS A **CORE MODULE** THAT ALLOWS YOU TO **WORK WITH STREAMING DATA** — HANDLING DATA **PIECE BY PIECE**, INSTEAD OF LOADING IT ALL AT ONCE INTO MEMORY.

THIS IS ESPECIALLY USEFUL FOR:

* READING/WRITING LARGE FILES
* SENDING/RECEIVING DATA OVER THE NETWORK
* PROCESSING REAL-TIME AUDIO/VIDEO
* EFFICIENT MEMORY USAGE

---

## 🔹 WHAT IS A STREAM?

A **STREAM** IS AN **ABSTRACT INTERFACE** FOR WORKING WITH DATA THAT FLOWS OVER TIME. STREAMS ARE **EVENT-BASED** AND ALLOW **ASYNCHRONOUS** READING/WRITING OF DATA.

THERE ARE **FOUR MAIN TYPES** OF STREAMS IN NODE.JS:

| TYPE        | DESCRIPTION                         | EXAMPLE                   |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| `READABLE`  | STREAM FROM WHICH DATA CAN BE READ  | READING A FILE            |
| `WRITABLE`  | STREAM TO WHICH DATA CAN BE WRITTEN | WRITING TO A FILE         |
| `DUPLEX`    | BOTH READABLE AND WRITABLE          | TCP SOCKETS               |
| `TRANSFORM` | MODIFY DATA AS IT IS WRITTEN/READ   | COMPRESSING/DECOMPRESSING |

---

## 🔹 IMPORTING THE `STREAM` MODULE

```JS
CONST STREAM = REQUIRE('STREAM');
```

BUT OFTEN, YOU USE IT VIA MODULES LIKE `FS`, `HTTP`, ETC., WHICH ARE BUILT ON TOP OF THE `STREAM` MODULE.

---

## 🔹 EXAMPLE: READING A FILE WITH STREAMS

```JS
CONST FS = REQUIRE('FS');

CONST READABLESTREAM = FS.CREATEREADSTREAM('LARGEFILE.TXT', { ENCODING: 'UTF8' });

READABLESTREAM.ON('DATA', (CHUNK) => {
    CONSOLE.LOG('RECEIVED CHUNK:', CHUNK);
});

READABLESTREAM.ON('END', () => {
    CONSOLE.LOG('FINISHED READING FILE.');
});

READABLESTREAM.ON('ERROR', (ERR) => {
    CONSOLE.ERROR('ERROR:', ERR.MESSAGE);
});
```

> THIS READS THE FILE **IN CHUNKS**, NOT ALL AT ONCE — WHICH IS MEMORY-EFFICIENT.

---

## 🔹 EXAMPLE: WRITING TO A FILE WITH STREAMS

```JS
CONST FS = REQUIRE('FS');

CONST WRITABLESTREAM = FS.CREATEWRITESTREAM('OUTPUT.TXT');

WRITABLESTREAM.WRITE('HELLO, THIS IS LINE 1\N');
WRITABLESTREAM.WRITE('THIS IS LINE 2\N');

WRITABLESTREAM.END('FINISHED WRITING.\N');
```

---

## 🔹 EXAMPLE: PIPE – CONNECTING READABLE AND WRITABLE STREAMS

```JS
CONST FS = REQUIRE('FS');

CONST READABLE = FS.CREATEREADSTREAM('INPUT.TXT');
CONST WRITABLE = FS.CREATEWRITESTREAM('COPIED.TXT');

READABLE.PIPE(WRITABLE);
```

> THE `PIPE()` METHOD **AUTOMATICALLY READS** FROM THE SOURCE AND **WRITES** TO THE DESTINATION.

---

## 🔹 TRANSFORM STREAM EXAMPLE

```JS
CONST { TRANSFORM } = REQUIRE('STREAM');

CONST UPPERCASETRANSFORM = NEW TRANSFORM({
  TRANSFORM(CHUNK, ENCODING, CALLBACK) {
    CONST UPPER = CHUNK.TOSTRING().TOUPPERCASE();
    CALLBACK(NULL, UPPER);
  }
});

PROCESS.STDIN.PIPE(UPPERCASETRANSFORM).PIPE(PROCESS.STDOUT);
```

> TYPE SOMETHING IN TERMINAL — IT WILL BE CONVERTED TO UPPERCASE USING A TRANSFORM STREAM.

---

## 🔹 REAL-WORLD USE CASES

* SERVING LARGE FILES LIKE PDFS OR VIDEOS
* LOGGING SYSTEM (STREAM LOGS TO A FILE)
* BUILDING CLI TOOLS (USING `STDIN`, `STDOUT`)
* UPLOAD/DOWNLOAD SYSTEMS
* AUDIO/VIDEO PROCESSING PIPELINES

---

## ✅ ADVANTAGES OF STREAMS

* **EFFICIENT MEMORY USAGE**
* **NON-BLOCKING**, ASYNC HANDLING
* **BUILT-IN BACKPRESSURE** MANAGEMENT (ADJUSTS DATA FLOW RATE AUTOMATICALLY)

